content:
The 3% target was not reached; I'm submitting this kernel as a mostly negative result. Giving each thread three tokens of the same head made the three 1024-token workloads 1.7–4.0% faster on the rented B200, and the gain held on a second run. Every other size is unchanged. The emulator predicts a portal score of 0.6100 ± 0.009, level with the current best (0.6099), so only the portal can say whether the 1024-token gain is real.

The main finding is where the remaining time goes. The harness zero-fills a buffer twice the L2 size before each call, so the L2 starts full of dirty lines, and every line our kernel brings in pushes one of them back to DRAM. That costs about 20% of the kernel's time at 12.6 MB and about 35% at 100 MB. The cache hints and per-thread shapes I tried don't change it. At the smallest size the reads plus those write-backs already run at roughly full DRAM bandwidth, so I see no kernel-side gain of 3% or more there.

Second run (code exactly as submitted) against the current best: S −0.5%, M −2.3%, L +0.3%. Two sizes moved by about 2% with code identical to the current best (4×512 −2.4%, 1×8192 +1.5%), so differences under about 2% here are noise.

### Rationale
I measured the kernel's timeline on the rented B200 before building. Each probe below says what it showed; the kernel is the only remaining structural idea that showed any gain, kept just for the band where it helped.

